When Ines Varga inherited her grandmother's cottage in the hills, she also inherited an orchard of forty trees, most of them half-dead. In her first spring she noticed that one tree, standing close to the garden wall, blossomed a week later than the rest. By autumn it was heavy with small, striped apples whose pink flesh tasted faintly of strawberries.

{{41}}

Varga's curiosity soon hardened into something closer to obsession. The written record was thin: a single nineteenth-century nursery catalogue and a few lines in an old parish magazine. The apple seemed to have vanished from the valley within a generation, and no one could say whether her tree was the genuine article or merely a distant cousin. If she wanted to be sure, she needed proof.

{{42}}

One did, as it happens. A national fruit collection in Kent had kept a single Lantern Pippin alive since 1952, tucked away in a corner of its test garden. Varga posted a leaf from her tree and settled down to wait. The results arrived in March.

{{43}}

Identity established, she faced a second problem. A single tree cannot feed a valley, and apple trees do not grow true from seed: plant a pip from a Lantern Pippin and the result will be something else entirely.

{{44}}

She found the people she needed in the village itself. Two retired farmers, who had learnt the craft as boys, were persuaded to dust off their knives and teach a handful of volunteers. They were sceptical at first, but they agreed to try.

{{45}}

It was the second attempt, with the young grafts kept in a heated shed until the weather turned, that worked. Today there are more than two hundred Lantern Pippins growing in gardens across the valley, and a local school has planted a row beside its playground.

{{46}}
